                  "type": "application",
                  "title": "Final Mastery Map: Everything You Now Know",
                  "content": "Twenty-eight lessons. One integrated language. This is what you carry forward.\n\n**Unit 1 — Word-building foundations (L1-5)** — You understand how every medical term is constructed from prefixes, roots, combining vowels, and suffixes. You can decode terms you have never seen before by identifying word parts. You know the rules for pronunciation, plural forms, and eponyms.\n\n**Unit 2 — Body system terminology, Part 1 (L6-11)** — Integumentary, musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, respiratory, gastrointestinal, urinary. The structural and functional terminology of the systems that present most often in any setting.\n\n**Unit 3 — Body system terminology, Part 2 (L12-17)** — Nervous, endocrine, reproductive, blood/lymphatic, special senses, mental health. The systems where specialty-specific terminology dominates.\n\n**Unit 4 — Body system terminology, Part 3 (L18-21)** — Diagnostic imaging, pharmacology, surgical/procedural, oncology. The cross-cutting domains that appear in every chart.\n\n**Unit 5 — Clinical documentation and abbreviations (L22-25)** — Reading H&P, operative report, discharge summary, pathology, consultation. Decoding the abbreviations that dominate every section of every record.\n\n**Unit 6 — Advanced applications and capstone (L26-28)** — Multisystem disease integration, HIM operational terminology, and this comprehensive mastery challenge.\n\n**The connection to your career:** Every credential — RHIA, RHIT, CCS, CCS-P, CCA, CDIP, CHPS, CPHI, CTR — requires this terminology as foundation. Every HIM role — coder, CDI specialist, privacy officer, registrar, analyst, informaticist — uses it daily. Every adjacent course in your HIM curriculum — HITT 1301, 1341, 1342, 2246, 2335 — builds on it.\n\n**One last principle:** Medical terminology is a living language. New diseases (COVID-19, monkeypox), new drugs (CAR-T therapies, GLP-1 agonists), new technologies (robotic surgery, AI diagnostics) bring new terminology every year. The vocabulary you have learned is not a fixed set to memorize — it is a system of word parts you can apply forever. Master the system, and any new term that ever appears in any chart you ever touch is one you can decode.\n\nYou are now medically literate.",
